    "short_description": "Reshape, filter, and enrich events as they pass through your pipeline.",
    "body": "<h1>Transforming events in flight</h1>

{{ds:component/docs-toc|items_html=<li><a href="#reshaping-events">Reshaping events</a></li><li><a href="#filtering-events">Filtering events</a></li><li><a href="#enriching-events">Enriching events</a></li><li><a href="#chaining-transforms">Chaining transforms</a></li><li><a href="#monitoring-transformations">Monitoring transformations</a></li>}}

<p>Once events enter a pipeline, you can reshape their structure, filter out unwanted records, and enrich them with data from external sources — all before they reach the destination. This guide covers the three main transformation patterns.</p>

<h2 id="reshaping-events">Reshaping events</h2>
<p>A reshape transformation maps fields from the source schema into a new output schema. This is useful when your destination expects a different structure than your source provides, or when you want to rename or reorganize fields.</p>

{{ds:component/code-block|filename=reshape-transform.json|language=json|code={\\n  \"type\": \"reshape\",\\n  \"input_schema\": {\\n    \"customer_id\": \"string\",\\n    \"order_amount\": \"number\",\\n    \"timestamp\": \"string\"\\n  },\\n  \"mappings\": {\\n    \"customer_id\": \"customerId\",\\n    \"order_amount\": \"totalPrice\",\\n    \"timestamp\": \"createdAt\"\\n  }\\n}}}

<p>You can also project only a subset of fields, drop sensitive data before it reaches the destination, or flatten nested structures.</p>

{{ds:component/alert|variant=info|title=Type coercion|body_html=Reshape transformations can perform basic type conversion: string to number, epoch milliseconds to ISO timestamp, and boolean from string "true"/"false".}}

<h2 id="filtering-events">Filtering events</h2>
<p>A filter transformation passes events through a condition and drops any that don't match. This prevents your destination from filling up with irrelevant records.</p>

{{ds:component/code-block|filename=filter-transform.json|language=json|code={\\n  \"type\": \"filter\",\\n  \"condition\": {\\n    \"field\": \"order_amount\",\\n    \"operator\": \"gte\",\\n    \"value\": 100\\n  }\\n}}}

<p>Filters support comparison operators (<code>eq</code>, <code>neq</code>, <code>gt</code>, <code>gte</code>, <code>lt</code>, <code>lte</code>), string operations (<code>contains</code>, <code>starts_with</code>, <code>ends_with</code>), and logical combinations using <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, and <code>not</code>.</p>

{{ds:component/alert|variant=warning|title=Filtered events are dropped|body_html=An event that fails a filter is silently discarded — it doesn't retry, doesn't go to the dead-letter queue, and is not sent to the destination. Use filters when you're confident you want to discard non-matching records.}}

<h2 id="enriching-events">Enriching events</h2>
<p>An enrichment transformation fetches additional data from an external source (a database, API, or reference table) and adds it to the event payload. This is useful for adding context that the source system doesn't provide.</p>

{{ds:component/code-block|filename=enrich-transform.json|language=json|code={\\n  \"type\": \"enrich\",\\n  \"source\": {\\n    \"type\": \"http\",\\n    \"url\": \"https://api.example.com/customers/{customer_id}\",\\n    \"auth\": \"bearer\"\\n  },\\n  \"output_field\": \"customer_details\"\\n}}}

<p>Enrichments extract fields from the event (like a customer ID) to look up external data, then add the response to the event before sending

